WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.072
Pace rollout by consequence and reversibility.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:08.497
Start with a permitted boundary, observe
exceptions, and retain a known manual path.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:13.345
Define triggers for disable, rollback,
and escalation before the incident.

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:16.466
A backup is useful only if restoration works.

00:00:16.536 --> 00:00:22.851
For production, evaluate restoration, retention,
monitoring, identity, and operating support

00:00:22.921 --> 00:00:24.563
in the actual environment.

00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:30.052
The course prototype intentionally demonstrates
the questions without claiming all those

00:00:30.122 --> 00:00:31.637
controls are complete.

00:00:31.707 --> 00:00:34.693
The API becomes unavailable during a shift.

00:00:34.763 --> 00:00:37.131
Agents can still use the category sheet.

00:00:37.201 --> 00:00:43.217
Maya disables assistance, Luis diagnoses the
service, and the event enters the learning log.

00:00:43.287 --> 00:00:46.701
The team can continue operating
while the feature recovers.

00:00:46.771 --> 00:00:51.549
Pause the video and complete this exercise
before opening the worked response.

00:00:51.619 --> 00:00:57.209
Write a recovery trigger, immediate action,
responsible owner, and evidence required to

00:00:57.279 --> 00:00:58.281
resume.

00:00:58.351 --> 00:01:02.191
Trigger: repeated failed requests
or an unauthorized action.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:06.315
Immediate action: disable
assistance and use manual review.

00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:10.950
Owner: Maya for workflow continuity
and Luis for technical recovery.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.951
Resume only after the relevant fault is corrected,
boundary tests pass, and the owner confirms

00:01:17.021 --> 00:01:23.933
readiness. A production recovery drill would need the
real environment and approved operating procedures.

00:01:24.003 --> 00:01:26.093
What should be defined before rollout?

00:01:26.163 --> 00:01:29.321
A. Disable triggers and a manual fallback B.

00:01:29.391 --> 00:01:31.823
A guaranteed zero-failure promise C.

00:01:31.893 --> 00:01:34.794
An assumption that the
owner is always available.

00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:36.315
The best answer is A.

00:01:36.385 --> 00:01:38.838
Recovery design is part of readiness.
